.TH REROUTED 1 2026-11-18 "rerouted" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rerouted \- the reference Tieroute homeserver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rerouted
.RB [ \-env
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B rerouted
.RB [ \-env
.IR FILE ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rerouted
is the reference homeserver for Tieroute, a federated, end-to-end encrypted chat protocol.
With no command, it runs the server until it gets SIGINT or SIGTERM.
With a command, it sets up, checks, backs up, or administers a homeserver, and exits.
.PP
Commands that only look at the database open it read-only, and never upgrade it, so they're
safe beside a running server of another version.
Commands that read the data directory need the user rerouted runs as, or root:
.BR "sudo \-u rerouted rerouted status" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-env " FILE"
The settings file. See
.B SETTINGS FILE
for where rerouted looks without it. A file named here must exist.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the commands and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR init " [\fIflags\fR]"
Set up a new homeserver: write the settings file (never over an existing one), create the
data directory and the server's keys, print the DNS records to add, and add the sites to
Caddy if it's installed. It asks for what it needs, or takes flags:
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
The server's DNS name. Required with
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.BI \-\-handles " DOMAIN"
The domain handles go under. Default: the name.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " HOST:PORT"
The local address. Default: the first free port from 8080 on 127.0.0.1.
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-dir " DIR"
The data directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-registration " invite|public|closed"
Who can sign up. Default: invite.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " FILE"
The settings file to write.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Ask nothing: take the flags and the defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-caddy
Leave Caddy alone.
.TP
.B \-\-caddy\-only
Only add an existing settings file's sites to Caddy.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-updates
Turn on automatic updates.
.TP
.B \-\-start
Enable and start the systemd service.
.RE
.IP
As root, init writes
.I /etc/rerouted/rerouted.env
with data in
.IR /var/lib/rerouted ,
for
.BR rerouted.service .
If that file exists, it sets up a second instance:
.I /etc/rerouted/NAME.env
with data in
.IR /var/lib/rerouted\-NAME ,
for
.BR rerouted@NAME.service .
.IP
Caddy's configuration is never replaced. The sites go in
.IR /etc/caddy/rerouted/NAME.caddy ,
the on-demand TLS ask in
.IR /etc/caddy/rerouted/global/NAME.caddy ,
and the Caddyfile gains two import lines, after a backup to
.IR Caddyfile.bak\-rerouted\-TIME .
If
.B caddy validate
fails, init restores the Caddyfile and removes what it added.
.TP
.BR checkup " [\fB\-\-peer\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-handle\fR \fIHANDLE\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIDURATION\fR]"
Check the settings, disk space, the local listener, DNS, the server document over HTTPS and
its certificate, the clock, the call relay, and optionally a handle's certificate and
federation with another server. Prints PASS, WARN, FAIL, or INFO for each check.
.TP
.BR status " [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Show whether the server is running, its uptime, version, voice calls, outbox, pending
restart, and available update, and count the identities, devices, and communities in the
database.
.TP
.B config check
Check the settings file: errors that stop rerouted from starting, and likely mistakes, such
as settings that aren't rerouted's.
.TP
.BR backup " [\fIDEST\fR]"
Write the database, keys, files, and settings file to
.IR rerouted\-backup\-NAME\-TIME.tar.gz ,
mode 0600, in the directory
.I DEST
(default: the working directory), or to
.I DEST
if it ends in .tar.gz. Safe while the server runs.
.TP
.BR restore " \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]"
Restore a backup into the data directory. Refuses while the server runs, and over an
existing database unless
.B \-\-force
is given, which first moves the current data to
.I before\-restore\-TIME
in the data directory.
.TP
.BR accounts " [\fIN\fR]"
List the N newest identities whose chains this server stores (default 50).
.TP
.B communities
List the community servers this server hosts, with member counts.
.TP
.BR reports " [\fIN\fR]"
Print the N newest reports (default 20).
.TP
.BI remove\-media " ID " \-\-confirm
Delete a reported file. Fetches of it then answer 410 removed.
.TP
.BI release\-handle " HANDLE " \-\-confirm
Free a handle so a new identity can register it.
.TP
.BI keep\-handle " HANDLE"
Keep a handle reserved after its 30-day hold ends.
.TP
.BR auto\-updates " on|off|status"
Turn daily updates of the rerouted package on or off, through
.BR rerouted\-update.timer ,
or show their state. Only for package installs; not available on Arch Linux or Gentoo.
.TP
.B version
Print the version, Go version, platform, database schema version, and wire versions.
.TP
.BR completion " bash|zsh|fish"
Print a shell completion script.
.TP
.B help
Print the commands.
.SH SETTINGS FILE
Without
.BR \-env ,
rerouted uses the first of these that exists:
.I .env
in the working directory, then
.IR /etc/rerouted/rerouted.env .
If neither exists, settings come from the environment only. Variables already set in the
environment win over the file. Each line is
.IR KEY = value ;
lines starting with # are comments.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the settings file. Settings that need a restart are logged.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Restart once no voice calls are active: rerouted exits with status 75, for the service
manager to start it again.
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Shut down.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command or check fails, 2 for a usage or settings error, and 75 when
the server exits to be restarted (SIGUSR2).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/rerouted/rerouted.env
The packages' settings file.
.TP
.I /etc/rerouted/NAME.env
Settings for the instance
.BR rerouted@NAME.service .
.TP
.I /var/lib/rerouted
The packages' data directory.
.TP
.I DATA_DIR/rerouted\-v1.db
The database, with its \-wal and \-shm files.
.TP
.I DATA_DIR/server\-key.json\fR, \fIDATA_DIR/server\-key\-v2.json
The server's signing keys for wire versions 1 and 2.
.TP
.I DATA_DIR/media/
Uploaded files, end-to-end encrypted.
.TP
.I DATA_DIR/turn\-secret
The built-in call relay's secret.
.TP
.I DATA_DIR/status.json
The running server's status, read by
.BR "rerouted status" .
.TP
.I /etc/caddy/rerouted/
The Caddy sites and on-demand TLS ask that
.B rerouted init
writes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR caddy (1),
.BR systemctl (1).
The guides in the source:
.IR docs/run\-a\-homeserver.md ,
.IR docs/administer\-a\-homeserver.md ,
and
.IR docs/settings.md .
